# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-economy-objection.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1 has "accounted_without_god → ¬need_god"; aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1.3 have "superfluous_many"; they differ when only "everything we see in the world can be accounted for by other principles, supposing God did not exist" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-economy-objection.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "everything we see in the world can be accounted for by other principles, supposing God did not exist" is true and "it is superfluous to suppose that what can be accounted for by a few principles has been produced by many" and "there is need to suppose God's existence" are false. Is S2, "2: Further, it is superfluous to suppose that what can be accounted for by a few principles has been produced by many.", true then?
aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1 has "natural_reduced ∧ voluntary_reduced → accounted_without_god"; aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-economy-objection.logic.r1.3 have "natural_reduced ∧ voluntary_reduced → accounted_without_god" and "superfluous_many ∧ accounted_without_god → ¬need_god"; they differ when only "it is superfluous to suppose that what can be accounted for by a few principles has been produced by many", "everything we see in the world can be accounted for by other principles, supposing God did not exist", "all natural things can be reduced to one principle which is nature", "all voluntary things can be reduced to one principle which is human reason, or will" and "there is need to suppose God's existence" are true, which the first allows
